<p>The City of Corpus Christi, Nueces County, Port Aransas, Portland, Aransas Pass, Rockport, and the surrounding counties all participate in the NFIP. Some properties in V, VE, or V1-30 flood zones that were built or substantially changed on or after September 1, 2009 must show proof of NFIP coverage, when available, to get TWIA coverage (<a href="https://www.twia.org/coverage-eligibility/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TWIA eligibility</a>).</p>

<h2>Storm Surge and Flash Flooding on the Coastal Bend</h2>
<p>Surge has repeatedly reached Coastal Bend properties. NOAA records describe storm tides of 6 to 8 feet in Port Aransas during Hurricane Harvey in 2017, and a storm tide of around 4 feet from Ingleside On-the-Bay to Aransas Pass. During Hurricane Hanna in 2020, surge damaged parts of the Corpus Christi Marina and piers along the bay and inundated North Beach. Even weaker systems push water onto the islands; Tropical Storm Harold in 2023 raised tides around Corpus Christi and Nueces bays and at Whitecap Beach on north Padre Island.</p>
<p>Inland, flash flooding is common. NOAA's Storm Events Database lists 256 flash flood reports for Nueces County alone, and slab-on-grade homes can take water quickly when streets and drainage back up. Our <a href="/storm-history/">storm history</a> page has more local detail.</p>

<h2>Wind vs. Water Disputes After a Hurricane</h2>
<p>When a hurricane brings both wind and surge, the wind insurer (your homeowners carrier or TWIA) and the flood insurer may each point to the other. A wall damaged by wind and then flooded can look, after the water recedes, like a flood-only loss. Interior water stains can come from wind-driven rain through a damaged roof or from rising water. The two claims usually have to be documented separately and carefully.</p>
<p>Evidence that often helps includes measured and photographed interior and exterior water lines, roof and opening damage showing how rain got in from above, NOAA tide gauge data from stations such as Packery Channel, Port Aransas, or Rockport, and timeline evidence of when wind peaked compared with when water arrived. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage claims</a> goes deeper, and our <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> page covers the wind side.</p>

