<h2>Documenting a Flood Claim</h2>
<p>Flood claims depend heavily on documentation. Before cleanup, and continuing as the property is dried out:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photograph and video</strong> every room, the water line on walls, and the outside of the building.</li>
<li><strong>Measure water depth</strong> inside and outside, and note how long water stood.</li>
<li><strong>Inventory contents</strong> with descriptions, ages, quantities, and replacement prices; photograph items before disposal and keep samples of flooring and carpet when practical.</li>
<li><strong>Keep receipts</strong> for water extraction, drying equipment, debris removal, and temporary repairs.</li>
<li><strong>Collect your policy and declarations page</strong> so building and contents limits and deductibles are clear.</li>
<li><strong>Note deadlines and instructions</strong> from your flood insurer, including any proof of loss requirements.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help you organize the file.</p>

<h2>First Steps After Floodwater Recedes</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Avoid standing water, which can hide debris and carry electrical hazards, and do not return until officials say it is safe.</li>
<li><strong>Report the flood claim</strong> to your flood insurer as soon as you can, and report any wind damage separately to your homeowners insurer or TWIA.</li>
<li><strong>Document before you clean.</strong> Photograph water lines, damaged rooms, and contents before anything is moved.</li>
<li><strong>Start drying out.</strong> Remove standing water and wet materials when it is safe, and keep receipts for equipment and labor. Wet drywall, insulation, and carpet can grow mold within days in Coastal Bend humidity.</li>
<li><strong>Keep samples and records</strong> of removed flooring, cabinets, and drywall, along with notes on what was thrown out and why.</li>
<li><strong>Track every deadline</strong> and instruction your flood insurer gives you.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What Flood Policies Often Limit</h2>
<p>Flood policies are not the same as homeowners policies. Flood policies can limit what is covered in certain parts of a building, which matters for elevated homes on the islands with enclosures or storage underneath; check how your policy treats those areas. Contents coverage is separate from building coverage and has its own limit. Some items may be valued at actual cash value rather than replacement cost. Mold that develops because water was not removed promptly may be disputed. Read your policy, or ask us to walk through it with you. Our article on <a href="/water-damage-vs-flood-damage-corpus-christi/">water damage vs. flood damage</a> explains where the line usually falls.</p>

<h2>How a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster Can Help</h2>
<p>A Corpus Christi Public Adjuster represents the insured only. On a flood loss, we can help document the damage, prepare a detailed building estimate and contents inventory, organize photos and water-depth records, and keep a related wind claim with your homeowners insurer or TWIA moving at the same time. We do not overstate what is possible: NFIP and private flood policies set their own rules, coverage is limited to what the policy provides, and we cannot promise any outcome. For legal questions about a flood claim dispute or deadline to sue, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
<p>If water entered from a burst pipe, appliance leak, or roof opening rather than rising water, the claim usually belongs under your property policy instead; see <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a>.</p>
